.TH MGREP 1 "August 2026" "mgrep" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mgrep \- fast, multithreaded fixed-string search
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mgrep
.RI [ OPTIONS ] " PATTERN " [ PATH ...]
.br
.B mgrep
.RI [ OPTIONS ] " PATTERN " \-
.br
.B mgrep
.RI [ "FILTER OPTIONS" ] " --files " [ PATH ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mgrep
searches files or standard input for a fixed string.
It is designed around fast file discovery, literal matching, and batched output.
Matching is not regular-expression based.
.PP
Without source or line-number output options, normal file searches print the path
of each file containing a match. Options such as
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-o ,
and
.B \-O
select more detailed output.
.PP
If
.I PATH
is
.BR \- ,
standard input is searched at that point in the operand sequence. If no path is
given and standard input is redirected, standard input is searched automatically.
Multiple paths, file lists, and standard-input operands may be combined.
.SH PATTERN
Patterns are fixed strings. By default, mgrep decodes
.BR \en ,
.BR \er ,
.BR \et ,
.BR \e0 ,
.BR \e\e ,
and
.BI \ex NN
escapes before searching. Other escaped characters lose the backslash and are
searched literally. Use
.B --literal
to preserve backslashes exactly.
.PP
Matching is byte-oriented. The
.B --ignore-case
option folds ASCII letters only.
.SH SEARCH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " --help
Display concise command help and exit successfully.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " --invert-match
Select lines that do not contain the pattern. This cannot be combined with
.BR --only-matching .
.TP
.BR \-i ", " --ignore-case
Match ASCII letters case-insensitively.
.TP
.B \-r
Recursively search below directory operands. Without this option, directory
handling is limited to the requested directory level.
.TP
.BI \-m " NUM"
Read at most
.I NUM
lines from each input. A value of zero disables the limit.
.TP
.B --literal
Treat every pattern backslash literally instead of decoding pattern escapes.
.SH OUTPUT OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " --count
Print the number of selected lines for each input that has at least one selected
line. Count output takes precedence over normal, one-line, and only-matching output.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " --quiet
Print nothing and stop as soon as the requested search status is known. Exit status
still indicates whether a match was found. Quiet mode takes precedence over other
output modes.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " --one-line
Print each selected source line as one physical output line. File records contain
the path, an optional line number, and a match-centered source excerpt. Output is
capped at 100 visible ASCII columns; ANSI control sequences do not count toward
the limit. Long paths and source excerpts are marked with an ellipsis.
.IP
One-line mode cannot be combined with
.BR \-B ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR --heading ,
.BR --only-matching ,
or a pattern containing a decoded newline.
.TP
.BR \-O ", " --only-matching
Print only the matching text, once per non-overlapping occurrence. File output
includes its path; add
.B \-l
to include line numbers. This cannot be combined with
.BR --invert-match .
.TP
.B \-l
Include the matching line number in output.
.TP
.B \-s
Print the source line containing each selected match.
.TP
.BI \-B " NUM"
Print up to
.I NUM
lines before each selected line.
.TP
.BI \-A " NUM"
Print up to
.I NUM
lines after each selected line.
.TP
.B \-n
Print an additional newline after each result.
.TP
.B --heading
Group line and source output below one heading for each matching file.
.TP
.B --verbose
Print completion and match statistics after searching. Quiet mode suppresses this
summary.
.SH INPUT AND FILTER OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
Search all files. This permits binary-looking input and disables the normal ignore
rules and extension-based skips. It may substantially increase work.
.TP
.B --files
List the files mgrep would search instead of searching their contents. No pattern
is required. With no path operand, this mode recursively lists from the current
directory.
.TP
.BR --ff " " FILE ", " --files-from " " FILE
Read newline-delimited input paths from
.IR FILE .
Use
.B \-
as the file-list name to read the path list from standard input.
.TP
.BR --files-from0 " " FILE ", " --null-files-from " " FILE
Read NUL-delimited input paths from
.IR FILE .
The NUL form supports paths containing newlines.
.TP
.BI --type " TYPE"
Search only files belonging to a named type. Supported names are
.BR header ,
.BR headers ,
.BR source ,
.BR sources ,
.BR cpp ,
and
.BR c++ .
.TP
.BI --ext " EXT[,EXT...]"
Search only files whose extensions occur in the comma-separated list. A leading
dot is optional.
.TP
.BI --glob " GLOB"
Search only paths matching
.IR GLOB .
The matcher supports
.BR * ,
.BR ? ,
and recursive
.B **
components.
.TP
.BI --exclude-glob " GLOB"
Skip paths matching
.IR GLOB .
May be specified more than once.
.TP
.B --
End option parsing. This permits patterns or paths beginning with a hyphen.
.SH APPEARANCE OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " --pretty
Compatibility alias. ANSI colors are enabled by default.
.TP
.BR \-t " THEME" ", " --theme " THEME"
Select a color theme. Supported themes are
.BR blue ,
.BR red ,
.BR green ,
.BR purple ,
.BR cyan ,
.BR yellow ,
.BR orange ,
.BR pink ,
.BR mono ,
.BR bright ,
.BR gruvbox ,
.BR nord ,
.BR dracula ,
and
.BR nebula .
.TP
.B --no-color
Disable ANSI color output, including concise help when specified before
.BR \-h .
.TP
.BI --colors " COMPONENT:ATTR:VALUE"
Override one color attribute. Components are
.BR path ,
.BR file ,
.BR line ,
.BR match ,
and
.BR source .
Attributes are
.BR fg ,
.BR bg ,
and
.BR style .
Foreground and background values may be a number from 0 through 255 or one of
.BR black ,
.BR red ,
.BR green ,
.BR yellow ,
.BR blue ,
.BR magenta ,
.BR purple ,
.BR cyan ,
.BR white ,
.BR gray ,
or
.BR grey .
Styles are
.BR bold ,
.BR dim ,
.BR italic ,
.BR underline ,
and
.BR reverse .
The option may be repeated. For example:
.PP
.RS
.B --colors match:fg:magenta --colors source:fg:cyan
.RE
.SH DEFAULT FILE SELECTION
Unless
.B \-a
is used, mgrep skips binary-looking input, hidden directories, common build and
cache directories, archives, media, object files, and files without extensions.
It also loads ignore rules from
.IR $HOME/.ignore
when that file exists. Explicit file operands remain subject to active type,
extension, and glob filters.
.SH HOME IGNORE RULES
During normal searches, mgrep reads
.IR $HOME/.ignore .
Leading and trailing whitespace is removed. Empty lines, comments beginning with
.BR # ,
and negation rules beginning with
.B !
are ignored. Negation is not currently supported.
.PP
A plain name skips files and directories with that basename. A trailing slash
makes a rule directory-only. A leading
.B *
creates a basename suffix rule, such as
.B *.generated
or
.BR *cache/ .
Directory prefixes are discarded, and rules containing
.B ?
or
.B [
are ignored. This is intentionally a limited subset rather than full gitignore
syntax.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Locates the optional
.I .ignore
file used by normal searches.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one match was found, concise help was displayed, or
.B --files
completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
The search completed successfully but found no matches.
.TP
.B 2
An error occurred, such as invalid options, an invalid option combination, a
missing pattern, an invalid path, or an unreadable file list.
.PP
With
.BR --quiet ,
a found match may take precedence over errors encountered later because the search
stops as soon as success is known.
.SH EXAMPLES
Recursively find files containing a fixed string:
.PP
.RS
.B "mgrep -r 'int main(' ."
.RE
.PP
Print matching source with line numbers:
.PP
.RS
.B mgrep -rls needle src
.RE
.PP
Print compact one-line records:
.PP
.RS
.B mgrep -rol needle src
.RE
.PP
Print each matched occurrence:
.PP
.RS
.B mgrep -rO needle src
.RE
.PP
Search redirected standard input without colors:
.PP
.RS
.B command | mgrep --no-color needle
.RE
.PP
List C and C++ source files mgrep would search:
.PP
.RS
.B mgrep --files --type source .
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $HOME/.ignore
Optional user ignore rules loaded during normal file searches.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR grep (1),
.BR rg (1)
.SH AUTHORS
Michael Haring and contributors.
